Z||000002||batboy||03-25-2001||02:30 PM||||The Fiero deity requires that the fair maiden pose in a short skirt next to their Fiero for a pic to be posted in the forum in order for the candle trick to work. Hmmm, I bet she's too smart to fall for that line of crap...LOL.<P>Have you removed the inner door panel and the plastic plugs at the trailing edge of the door. If you have small hands (which of course you do) you should be able to contort yourself into a position to reach it with only minimal loss of skin being scraped off your arms. Are you sure it's a screw, it might be a plastic anchor, in which case, some folks pop them off (they usually break) then reinstall with new clips.||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||03-25-2001||02:38 PM||||Nope||||reg||1
Z||000004||voyagerspe||03-25-2001||02:38 PM||||Pray to the Dremel god.<P>use a Dremel with the cut off wheel.  then use a new clip when you reinstall. btw you need to take off the interior door panel to reinstall.  <P>[This message has been edited by voyagerspe (edited 03-25-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by voyagerspe (edited 03-25-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000005||Tina||03-25-2001||02:47 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>The Fiero deity requires that the fair maiden pose in a short skirt next to their Fiero for a pic to be posted in the forum in order for the candle trick to work. Hmmm, I bet she's too smart to fall for that line of crap...LOL.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nice try   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I prefer my trusty pair a jeans.<P>Anyways, yes to inner door.<BR>No to being able to reach it.<BR>Perhaps doing a custom bend job on a screwdriver will get me there.<P>Now I have not yet taken the 87 door apart.<BR>Is the same clip underneath there, or is there a model year diff.?<BR>Since I got the molding itself off in on piece, can I just transfer it (84 molding) on to the 87 clip?<BR>Or is the case, that once the two are separated the clip is automaticly shot.<BR>And the screw holding the clip in place, is used to tighten the molding onto the door securely?<BR>(wobble-factor)<P>BTW Nice to see you back on board,Batboy.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tina (edited 03-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||03-25-2001||03:56 PM||||Tina, I just disassembled two doors yesterday and you will not be able to get to the screw from removing the inside panel as you can see in the picture.  The screw head is behind sheet metal of the door. The clip is molded into the molding so unless you glue it together you will need a new molding. You can get to it by partially removing the door skin. That would be removing the four plastic rivets and the rear torx screw on the latch side of the door. Hopes this helps.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P3250007.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P3250008.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||03-25-2001||06:42 PM||||Manicure by Fiero!<P>Thanks Tina, was hoping you didn't<BR>take offense to my crude teasing.||||reg||1
Z||000008||MrPBody||03-25-2001||06:53 PM||||Probably the quote right unquote way is to remove the door skin.  However if you're lucky you can remove the four plastic torx screws holding the skin to the rear edge of the door, and pry away the door skin enough to remove the molding screw (Phillips) with a short offset screwdriver.  It's another good use for Rodney Dickman's dew strip tool.  Pry gently or you'll bend the door skin.  It probably helps to remove the door handle.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||sqoach||03-25-2001||08:51 PM||||that's a very familiar sight. a while back that piece of trim on my driver's side fell off, and left the part you have pictured. MrPBody was nice enough to send me another one for free  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>all i did was take the four crappy-plastic screws holding the back of the door skin off. then i carefully held it back while unscrewing the trim piece with an angled phillips screwdriver. rodney's tool would have worked better, but at least i didn't break anything  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>good luck!<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||03-25-2001||10:09 PM||||If your careful and do a good bend job, use a pair of small channel locks and bend the door frame enough to access the screw head. It will bend back easily with some finishing love taps with a body hammer.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tina||03-25-2001||10:44 PM||||Victory  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>The candle is lit.<P>BLACK & DECKER<BR> <BR>Just drilled a hole straight through to the <BR>back of the screw.<BR>then took it out with butterknife.<BR>(The bending of only philips did not work out <BR>that well)<BR>Yeah, yeah I know.<BR>But all he good tools are neatly tucked away <BR>at his work.  grrrrrrr.<P>BTW thanks for the pics,on file now. <BR> <P>||||reg||1
